Hello Sundos,

How did you configure the timewindow argument in your GGIR script? When analyzing WW (wake to wake) estimates, it's important to note that days longer than 1440 minutes can occur. This definition is based on using wake-up times to determine the duration of the entire day. For instance, if an individual woke up at 8 AM on the first day and at 9 AM on the second day, their measured day would span 1500 minutes. If you prefer to keep the day constrained to 24 hours, you can set the timewindow argument to "MM" to use a midnight-to-midnight definition. Alternatively, you can adjust the fixed times to define a day differently (e.g., from noon to noon) using the dayborder argument.

Check how these arguments are configured in your script. The sum of the day and sleep should always amount to 1440 minutes by using the following arguments:

GGIR(
...
dayborder = 0,
timewindow = "MM"
)

Best,
Marcus

